            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For a richer pint, swap 2 to 4 oz of the milk for plain Greek yogurt or add a tablespoon of cream cheese; both add protein and body, and Greek yogurt adds a slight tang that suits berry and cheesecake flavors. For chocolate, a tablespoon of unsweetened cocoa deepens the flavor without adding much. Keep salt in mind too: a small pinch makes sweet flavors read sweeter when cold.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ratios also explain why one recipe works in one powder and fails in another. A 41 g, two-scoop serving with fiber and gum carries more solids than a 25 g scoop of plain isolate, so it freezes softer at the same milk volume. If you switch powders and the pint suddenly turns icy, either reduce the milk slightly or add a thickener. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-baking/">best protein powder for baking</a> guide uses the same logic for batters: casein and blends tolerate heat and liquid better than isolate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spinning-and-re-spinning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Spinning, Re-Spinning and Fixing a Powdery Pint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most Creami owners spin protein pints on the Lite Ice Cream program, which Ninja designed for lower-sugar and keto-style bases. The standard Ice Cream program is meant for full-sugar recipes and can leave a lean pint drier. Whichever you use, the first spin of a protein pint very often comes out powdery around the top and sides. That is normal and is not a sign the recipe failed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is the Re-spin button. Add a splash of milk, usually 1 to 2 tablespoons, make a small well in the center, and re-spin. Many pints need one re-spin, and a few need two. Add liquid a little at a time; too much turns the pint into a milkshake. If the outer edges stay icy after re-spinning, run a spoon around the inside of the pint to push them toward the center and spin again.
          </p>

          <p>
            Freezer temperature matters too. A very cold freezer makes a harder block, which the machine shaves into finer, drier powder. Some owners let the pint sit on the counter for 5 to 15 minutes before spinning, or run the outside under warm water for a minute, so the block softens slightly. That simple step often removes the need for a second re-spin. Never try to spin a pint that has frozen with a hump in the middle; flatten it first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix-ins come last. Use the Mix-in program after the texture is right, adding crushed cookies, chocolate chips, nuts or a protein bar cut into small pieces into a hole down the center. Leftovers can go back in the freezer, but they refreeze hard; plan to re-spin them before eating, with a splash of milk if needed.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
