        </div>

        <div id="clear-whey-sorbet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Clear Whey for Sorbet
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear whey isolate is the exception to everything above. It is made from acidified, hydrolyzed whey that dissolves into a thin, juice-like drink, with no creaminess at all. Put it in a milk base and the flavors clash; put it in water or a little juice and freeze, and the Creami's Sorbet program turns it into a light, icy, fruity dessert, closer to Italian ice than ice cream. For hot days or for people who find milky protein heavy, that is a genuinely useful option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear whey foams heavily when shaken or blended, so mix it gently, let it rest until the foam collapses, or skim the foam off before freezing. Some brands include an anti-foaming agent for this reason. Adding frozen fruit, a squeeze of lemon or lime, or a tablespoon of allulose softens the texture, because plain clear whey in water freezes very hard. A pint will almost always need a re-spin with a splash of water or juice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect less protein per pint than a creamy recipe, since there is no milk adding its own. One serving of a typical clear whey gives about 20 g of protein in roughly 80 to 100 calories, per labels at the time of writing. For many people that is an appealing trade: a large, cold, low-calorie dessert with a meaningful dose of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> guide compares the category in depth. For Creami sorbet specifically, the most important factors are a flavor you like cold, no off-notes when frozen, and a manageable foam. Fruity flavors such as lemonade, mango and watermelon translate best; the cola and cinnamon novelty flavors are hit and miss.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Buying on Amazon and Cost per Pint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every powder on this page was listed on Amazon US when we checked in November 2026, along with the Creami itself. As with any supplement on Amazon, buy from listings marked ships from and sold by Amazon, or from the brand's own storefront, rather than an unfamiliar third-party seller. Our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">guide to buying protein powder on Amazon</a> explains how to spot counterfeit tubs, why lot numbers and seals matter, and when Subscribe and Save is worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost per pint is mostly the powder. On the dated retailer prices in our label database, a serving ranged from roughly $1.24 for Quest at its brand site to about $2.04 for PEScience Select at Vitacost in early November 2026. Add milk and any pudding mix, and a pint usually lands somewhere around $2 to $3.50, well below the price of most branded high-protein ice creams. Prices move often, and Amazon's price on the day can be higher or lower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size matters for Creami owners in a way it does not for shake drinkers. If you make a pint most nights, you will go through a 2 lb tub in under a month, so the larger tubs and Subscribe and Save discounts add up quickly. On the other hand, a texture you dislike is expensive at 4 or 5 lb. Buy the smallest size first, confirm you like it frozen, then scale up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the machine. The original Creami models use 16 oz pints, while the Deluxe line uses larger 24 oz pints, so recipes need scaling by about half again. Pints are model-specific, so check compatibility before buying spares. If you also make shakes on the go, our sibling guide to the <a href="/best-portable-blender-for-protein-shakes/">best portable blender for protein shakes</a> covers the daytime half of the routine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not test these powders hands-on in a Creami. Instead, we started from the formula: each pick had to contain at least one of the things that make a frozen protein base work, meaning casein or milk protein, a gum or fiber system, or, for sorbet, a clear whey built for water. We read each label's protein blend and full ingredient list, preferring sources from our own label database and the brand's current listing, and we hedged every number because formulas vary by flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            We then weighed what Creami owners and recipe creators consistently report: which brands appear again and again in successful recipes, which flavors survive freezing, and which tubs get complaints about icy or gummy pints. We report that feedback as feedback, not as our own tasting. We also checked that each product was currently sold on Amazon US and that none appears in the avoid or limit groups of our heavy-metals guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            We gave weight to third-party testing where it exists, such as Informed Choice, and to price per serving. We left out products sold only through a single store or only in limited runs, and we did not include ready-made protein ice cream mixes, since their protein claims are harder to verify and they cost more than a tub of powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein ice cream is still a supplement-based food. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, have a milk allergy or take medication that interacts with diet, talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before making a protein pint a nightly habit. Sugar alcohols can cause digestive upset in larger amounts, and lactose-sensitive readers may prefer a whey isolate base or lactose-free milk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using plain whey isolate and water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A lean isolate mixed with water has almost nothing to soften the freeze, so it shaves into icy, chalky powder. If isolate is what you own, switch to milk, add 1/8 teaspoon of xanthan or guar gum or a spoon of pudding mix, and plan on a re-spin. Better still, mix half a scoop of casein into it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overdoing casein or gum</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                More thickener is not always better. A full scoop of casein in too little milk, or more than about 1/4 teaspoon of gum, can freeze gummy, doughy or slimy. Stay at the max fill line with enough liquid, start with small amounts of gum, and adjust one variable at a time so you know what changed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the re-spin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A powdery first spin is normal for protein pints and does not mean the recipe failed. Add one to two tablespoons of milk, make a small well in the center and press Re-spin. Most pints need one re-spin and some need two. Throwing the pint out or adding a big splash of liquid at once are both common mistakes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overfilling or freezing the pint unevenly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filling past the max line or freezing the pint on a slanted shelf can leave a hump that the blade struggles with, and Ninja's instructions warn against both. Freeze on a level surface for about 24 hours, flatten any bump before spinning, and let a very hard pint sit on the counter for a few minutes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a large tub before testing it frozen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A powder you love as a shake can taste flat or bitter frozen, especially if it uses stevia. Buy the smallest size, make two or three pints with your usual milk and add-ins, then commit to a bigger tub or Subscribe and Save. Buy from listings shipped and sold by Amazon or the brand storefront.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder for Ninja Creami?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, a whey-casein blend such as PEScience Select gives the creamiest result with the least effort, because the casein and guar gum thicken the base before freezing. Quest Protein Powder is a cheaper blend that works the same way. If you prefer a pure casein, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard Casein is the thickest option, best used with plenty of milk.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why is my Ninja Creami protein ice cream powdery?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Protein shakes contain very little sugar or fat, so they freeze into a hard block that the blade shaves into dry powder. Add one to two tablespoons of milk and press Re-spin, sometimes twice. For future pints, thicken the base with casein, a little xanthan gum or pudding mix, add allulose, and let the pint sit a few minutes before spinning.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I use whey protein in a Ninja Creami?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, but plain whey isolate tends to freeze icy and airy. Use milk rather than water, add a small amount of xanthan or guar gum or a tablespoon of sugar-free pudding mix, and expect to re-spin. A whey blend with gums, such as Premier Protein powder, or mixing half a scoop of casein into your whey, gives a creamier pint.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do I need pudding mix for Creami protein ice cream?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Sugar-free instant pudding mix is popular because its starch and thickener give a softer, creamier freeze and add flavor, but a powder with casein and gum often works without it. If you want to skip it, try a casein blend, a pinch of xanthan gum, a little Greek yogurt or a tablespoon of allulose instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Ninja Creami setting is best for protein ice cream?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most owners use the Lite Ice Cream program, which Ninja designed for lower-sugar and keto-style bases, then re-spin with a splash of milk if the first pass is powdery. Use the Sorbet program for clear whey sorbet made with water or juice. Add cookies, chips or other extras with the Mix-in program once the texture is right.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in a Ninja Creami protein pint?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on the powder and milk. One serving of powder adds about 20 to 30 g of protein, per typical labels. Ultra-filtered milk adds roughly 13 g per cup for common brands, so a 16 oz pint with a full serving and about one and a half cups of that milk can reach roughly 40 to 50 g.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is it safe to eat a protein pint every night?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most healthy adults, a pint built from a reputable powder and milk is simply another protein serving. Count it toward your daily protein total, watch sugar alcohols if they upset your stomach, and choose powders with third-party testing. People with kidney disease, a milk allergy or pregnancy should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
